{
  "requirement": "In Markdown, escaped roles and role-looking text in inline code, fenced code, or raw HTML MUST remain literal and MUST NOT count as references. Raw HTML means an HTML block, an HTML comment, or an inline HTML tag including its attributes; text between inline tags is ordinary Markdown. A `{req}` directive inside a fenced code example and a legacy inline `{req}` role MUST NOT define a requirement.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: the requirement is met.** The implementation covers every obligation and is marked completely. The tests cover each obligation, with only minor untested variants.\n\n**Implementation**\n\n- `protected_ranges` (src/lib.rs, `{impl}` marked) parses the Markdown with pulldown-cmark. It protects the ranges of these events:\n  - `Start(Tag::CodeBlock)`, which covers fenced and indented code.\n  - `Event::Code`, which covers inline code.\n  - `Event::Html`, which covers HTML blocks and block-level HTML comments.\n  - `Event::InlineHtml`, which covers inline tags including their attributes, and inline comments.\n- Text between inline tags is not in any of those ranges, so it stays ordinary Markdown. This matches the requirement's wording.\n- `reference_roles` skips any `{` that `is_escaped` (an odd number of preceding backslashes) or `is_protected`. It does this only when `markdown` is true, so plain-text and source files are unaffected.\n- `markdown_fences` in src/fenced.rs uses the same protected ranges, so fenced references inside code or raw HTML stay literal. It is marked `{impl}`.\n- `definition_blocks` ignores `:::{req}`, `:::{example}` and `:::{definition}` openers and closers that overlap a code-block range. A directive inside a fenced code example therefore defines nothing.\n- A legacy inline `{req}` role never defines a requirement. Only colon-fence openers are recognized as definitions, and nothing parses an inline `{req}`.\n- The helpers `overlaps_protected`, `is_protected` and `is_escaped` are all marked `{impl}`.\n- I found no other code that scans Markdown for references or definitions without these protections. The call sites are `reference_roles`, `markdown_fences`, `definition_blocks` and `text_references`, and each goes through the marked functions. Behavior beyond the requirement, such as protecting the `:phrase:` line of a term definition, is covered by other requirements the same functions mark (`definitions.declaration`).\n\n**Tests**\n\n- `code_examples_and_legacy_roles_remain_literal` (src/lib.rs) covers:\n  - an inline-code `{req}`;\n  - a `:::{req}` inside a fenced code block, indented three spaces;\n  - a legacy `{req}` role;\n  - a real directive that still renders.\n- `markdown_examples_and_escaped_references_stay_literal` checks that an inline-code role, a fenced-code role and a backslash-escaped role are all left in the text and none renders as a badge.\n- `roles_in_raw_html_stay_literal` covers:\n  - an HTML block;\n  - a standalone comment;\n  - an inline comment;\n  - a tag attribute;\n  - a role between `<em>` tags, which is counted;\n  - non-Markdown mode, where the roles are not protected.\n- `colon_directives_render_markdown_bodies_and_legacy_roles_stay_literal` checks that an inline legacy `{req}` role does not define a requirement and that a real directive does.\n- `references_in_requirement_bodies_are_scanned_as_markdown` checks that a fenced example inside a requirement body is not counted as a reference.\n- `source_references_are_covered_by_evidence_inputs` (src/evidence.rs) checks the end-to-end validation path. A role in inline code or a fenced block in a `.md` file is not reported as a reference to an unknown requirement.\n- `examples_are_distinct_definitions_and_tests_link_to_them` also checks that an `{example}` directive inside a fenced code block is not defined.\n\nIndented code blocks and tilde fences are not tested individually. They go through the same `Tag::CodeBlock` handling as the tested backtick fence, so this is a minor gap that does not affect the judgment.",
  "references": "63b1057575f66c017c80e801045abc7d1fc65b0f938067e240ad6373e54015e3",
